Hmm, but choosing which brick dominates is complex. Let me simplify to the issue's exact recipe applied per-brick but with state so that a flip is applied once per axis within this pass. Track whether this pass already resolved X and Y (a flag each). Then process bricks; for the first brick that triggers an X resolution, flip vx once and push out along X. Since we only flip each axis once, the "double apply" problem disappears — the second overlapping brick that also suggests vy flip won't flip again.

But if brick A suggests X resolution and brick B (overlapping in same pass) suggests Y resolution, we'd flip both — which is effectively a diagonal, acceptable.

The per-brick penetration recipe: for a single brick, axis with smaller penetration is the face. With two overlapping bricks, one may be vertical seam (top/bottom hit) and one horizontal seam (side hit). Better: take the global minimum penetration across all overlapping bricks and resolve along that axis once.

Let me take the global-min approach — it's cleanest and matches "apply once":

```
function resolveBrickCollision(ball, bricks) {
  let bestPen = Infinity;
  let chooseX = null;  // true => resolve along X, false => along Y
  let push = 0;        // direction +/-

  for (const brick of bricks) {
    if (!brick.alive) continue;
    if (!collides(ball, brick)) continue;

    const penL = ball.x + ball.r - brick.x;
    const penR = brick.x + brick.w - (ball.x - ball.r);
    const penT = ball.y + ball.r - brick.y;
    const penB = brick.y + brick.h - (ball.y - ball.r);
    const penX = Math.min(penL, penR);
    const penY = Math.min(penT, penB);

    if (penX < bestPen) { bestPen = penX; chooseX = true; push = penL < penR ? -1 : 1; }
    if (penY < bestPen) { bestPen = penY; chooseX = false; push = penT < penB ? -1 : 1; }
  }

  if (chooseX === null) return false; // no hit

  if (chooseX) {
    // push out horizontally and flip vx
    ball.x = ball.r > brickEdge... 
  }
}
```

Wait, need ball.x to push it out relative to the actual edge. Since we have push direction, we need the brick that defined the resolution to compute the boundary. Let me store the boundary. Actually, I can push out by setting ball.x using the chosen direction: if push = -1 (hit left side, ball must go left of brick's left edge): ball.x = brick.x - ball.r. If push = +1: ball.x = brick.x + brick.w + ball.r. Store the brick used.

This is getting complex but let me just write it carefully. I'll store the resolved brick reference too.

