`findFirstBrickContact`:
```
function findFirstBrickContact(ball, bricks, fromX, fromY) {
  // If no from-position, just do a discrete check at current position.
  const dx = ball.x - fromX;
  const dy = ball.y - fromY;
  const dist = Math.hypot(dx, dy) || 1e-9;
  const ux = dx / dist, uy = dy / dist;
  // march from start to current
  for (let d = 0; d <= dist; d += 0.25) {
    const x = fromX + ux * d;
    const y = fromY + uy * d;
    for (const brick of bricks) {
      if (!brick.alive) continue;
      if (circleHitsRect(x, y, ball.r, brick)) {
        return { x, y, brick };
      }
    }
  }
  // discrete fallback at current position
  for (const brick of bricks) {
    if (!brick.alive) continue;
    if (circleHitsRect(ball.x, ball.y, ball.r, brick)) return { x: ball.x, y: ball.y, brick };
  }
  return null;
}
```

Then bounce based on entry: since we know direction of travel (ux, uy), determine the face hit by the penetration recipe on that single brick (smaller penetration = face), flip and push out using that brick's boundary.

This is a substantial but correct implementation. However, I'm now concerned about scope creep and the "apply exactly once per axis / per pass when overlapping two bricks" requirement. With sweep, we find the FIRST contact brick and resolve against it only — one bounce per pass, satisfying "apply exactly once". Good. But if the ball is currently resting overlapping two bricks after the bounce, next frame the discrete fallback would resolve against the first overlapping brick again. That's okay because the push-out prevents re-overlap along that axis; the other brick (side seam) may still overlap though. Hmm.

